<!--
  Wazuh custom rules — MSc dissertation detection lab
  Canonical copy. Deploys to /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml on Blue (10.10.10.10).

  Rule IDs are allocated in RULE_ID_REGISTER.md. Never invent an ID — Wazuh uses the FIRST
  occurrence of a duplicate and silently ignores the rest.

  After deploying:
    sudo /var/ossec/bin/wazuh-analysisd -t      # must be clean
    sudo systemctl restart wazuh-manager

  Chain child rules from sysmon_eid1_detections — the group Sysmon EID 1 events land in AFTER
  matching a built-in detection rule. NOT sysmon_event1. Verify against the `groups` field of a
  real event in Discover.
-->

<group name="attack,discovery,local,">

  <!--
    T1087.001 Local Account Discovery via net.exe
    Custom rule ID 100200 | level 10

    Default Wazuh rule 92031 detects this at level 3 and maps it to the PARENT technique T1087.
    This rule adds sub-technique precision (T1087.001) at proportionate severity. That contrast is
    the dissertation's core argument in miniature.

    Two conditions, ANDed:
      1. originalFileName is net.exe or net1.exe — harder to evade than matching the image path,
         since renaming the binary does not change the PE version resource.
      2. commandLine contains an account/group enumeration subcommand.

    Condition 2 was added after review. Without it the rule fired on every net.exe invocation
    (net use, net start, net view, net share) and mapped all of them to T1087.001 — imprecise in
    exactly the dimension the thesis claims to improve, and it would have inflated the positive
    class in the ML dataset with routine drive mappings.
  -->
  <!--
    ⚠️ PRECISION FIX 2026-08-08, found while preparing T1136.001 Create Local Account.

    As written, this rule matched `net user <name> /add` because that command line contains "user" - so
    ACCOUNT CREATION would have been reported as ACCOUNT DISCOVERY. That is the same class of error this
    project measures in the default ruleset: right family, wrong technique. `net user X /add` is
    T1136.001, `net user X /delete` is T1531 Account Access Removal, and neither is discovery.

    The negated condition below excludes the modifying switches, leaving 100200 to match enumeration
    only. Verified safe for the existing T1087.001 result: its atomics run `net user`, `net localgroup`
    and `quser` with no /add, /delete or /active, so the recorded measurements are unchanged.

    Caught by asking "which existing rule will fire on the NEXT technique's commands?" before running it -
    the same cross-rule check that was added to the register after T1016.
  -->
  <rule id="100200" level="10">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)net1?\.exe</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\s+(user|localgroup|group|accounts)\b</field>
    <!-- /del is a valid abbreviation of /delete and net.exe accepts it. The first version of this
         negation matched only the long form, so `net user /del <name>` still fired 100200 and account
         DELETION was reported as account DISCOVERY. Observed twice in the T1136.001 baseline. Windows
         command-line switches are routinely abbreviated - match the short forms too. -->
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2" negate="yes">(?i)\s/(add|delete|del|active|act)\b</field>
    <description>T1087.001 Local Account Discovery via net.exe</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1087.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================================
    T1082 System Information Discovery
    Custom rule IDs 100230-100233 | level 8

    MEASURED BASELINE (2026-08-06, 5 detonation windows, 14 atomic tests, 146 alerts):
    stock Wazuh detected T1082 ZERO times. What fired instead was generic execution telemetry:
        92052  level 4  cmd.exe by abnormal parent            -> T1059.003
        92032  level 3  Suspicious Windows cmd shell exec     -> T1087 + T1059.003
        92027  level 4  Powershell spawned powershell         -> T1059.001
    So the default ruleset does not merely lose precision on T1082 - it MISATTRIBUTES the activity to
    T1087 Account Discovery and T1059.003 Command Shell, i.e. the wrong technique AND the wrong tactic
    (Execution rather than Discovery). An analyst following those alerts investigates the wrong
    behaviour. That is a stronger finding than T1087.001's parent-only mapping, and these rules are the
    corrective.

    LEVEL RATIONALE: 8, against 10 for T1087.001. Both are Discovery, but host enumeration
    (systeminfo, hostname, OS version) is far more common in routine administration than account
    enumeration, so a lower level is proportionate. Severity is a deliberate, defensible choice here,
    not a copy of the previous rule.

    DESIGN, following the lesson recorded in RULE_ID_REGISTER.md note 1: match on originalFileName
    (survives binary renaming, since the PE version resource is unchanged) AND constrain the command
    line wherever the tool is multi-purpose. wmic and reg.exe do many things that are not discovery;
    systeminfo and hostname do exactly one thing each and need no command-line condition.

    ⚠️ VERIFY BEFORE TRUSTING: originalFileName values are asserted from the PE version resources, not
    yet observed in this lab's telemetry. Detonate T1082 and confirm each rule fires. If 100230 does
    not fire, check the actual originalFileName in Discover and adjust - a rule that silently never
    matches is indistinguishable from a technique that is not detected, which has already cost this
    project a day.
    ===================================================================================================
  -->

  <rule id="100230" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^(systeminfo|hostname)\.exe$</field>
    <description>T1082 System Information Discovery via native host-information utility</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1082</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100231" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^wmic\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\b(os|bios|computersystem|csproduct|diskdrive|qfe|environment)\b</field>
    <description>T1082 System Information Discovery via WMIC host query</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1082</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100232" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^reg\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(MachineGuid|CurrentBuildNumber|ProductName|SystemBiosVersion|VideoBiosVersion|International)</field>
    <description>T1082 System Information Discovery via registry query for host identity</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1082</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Closes the cmdlet blind spot found in T1087.001: rule 100200 keys on net.exe and detected nothing
    from atomic T1087.001-9's Get-LocalUser / Get-LocalGroup, so a purely PowerShell adversary evaded
    it entirely. The same gap applies to T1082 tests 37 and 38 (Get-WinSystemLocale, Get-PSDrive).

    LIMITATION, and it is a real one: this only sees cmdlets passed on the command line via -Command
    or -File. It cannot see cmdlets typed into an interactive session, because no new process is
    created and EID 1 never fires. Full coverage requires PowerShell Script Block Logging (EID 4104) -
    which this endpoint's own SCA scan reports as NOT enabled ("Ensure 'Turn on PowerShell Script Block
    Logging' is set to 'Enabled'", CIS Win10 v4.0.0). Enabling it is a concrete, evidence-backed
    recommendation for the dissertation rather than an assumption.
  -->
  <rule id="100233" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^powershell(\.exe)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(Get-ComputerInfo|Get-WinSystemLocale|Get-PSDrive|Win32_OperatingSystem|Win32_ComputerSystem|Win32_BIOS|Win32_Processor)</field>
    <description>T1082 System Information Discovery via PowerShell cmdlet or WMI class</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1082</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================================
    T1033 System Owner/User Discovery
    Custom rule IDs 100240-100241 | level 8

    MEASURED BASELINE (2026-08-07, 5 windows, atomic tests 1/4/5/6/7, 165 attack alerts):
    the default ruleset detects T1033 only PARTIALLY, and this is a third distinct failure mode -
    different from T1087.001's parent-only mapping and T1082's outright misattribution.

      * `qwinsta.exe` session enumeration IS correctly detected: default rule 92022
        ("Gathered user information from Remote Desktop Service sessions") maps to T1033. 19 alerts.
      * `whoami.exe` - the single most common way anyone establishes identity on a host - produces 15
        alerts, ALL of them rule 92032, which maps to **T1087 + T1059.003**. So the most common T1033
        behaviour is attributed to Account Discovery and Command Shell instead.
      * The benign class fired NO T1033-mapped rule at all.

    So the gap is not the technique as a whole; it is the dominant variant within the technique.

    DELIBERATELY NOT WRITTEN, and each omission is itself a finding:
      * No rule for `qwinsta` / `quser` session enumeration - default rule 92022 already maps it to
        T1033 correctly. Adding one would duplicate correct behaviour and inflate the apparent
        contribution of the custom ruleset. Where the default is right, say so.
      * No rule for `wmic useraccount get /ALL` (present in atomic T1033-1). Enumerating local user
        accounts is arguably **T1087.001**, not T1033. ART places it under T1033, so the atomic spans
        two techniques - recorded as a limitation rather than papered over with a rule that would
        mislabel it either way.
    ===================================================================================================
  -->

  <rule id="100240" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^whoami\.exe$</field>
    <description>T1033 System Owner/User Discovery via whoami</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1033</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Closes the cmdlet path, as 100233 does for T1082. Atomic T1033-4 reads %USERNAME% via environment
    variables and T1033-5 calls [Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity]::GetCurrent() - neither spawns a
    distinctively named process, so nothing in the default ruleset attributes them to T1033.

    Same limitation as 100233: this only sees cmdlets passed via -Command or -File. Interactive use
    creates no new process and EID 1 never fires. Script Block Logging (EID 4104) is required for full
    coverage and the endpoint's own SCA scan reports it disabled.
  -->
  <rule id="100241" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^powershell(\.exe)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(WindowsIdentity|env:USERNAME|env:USERDOMAIN|::UserName|Get-LocalUser)</field>
    <description>T1033 System Owner/User Discovery via PowerShell identity enumeration</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1033</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================================
    T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery
    Custom rule IDs 100250-100252 | level 8

    MEASURED BASELINE (2026-08-07, 5 windows, atomic tests 1/2/9, 44 attack alerts):
    NOTHING in the default ruleset maps to T1016. What fires is:
        92032  L3  Suspicious Windows cmd shell execution   -> T1087 + T1059.003
        92052  L4  cmd prompt by abnormal parent            -> T1059.003
        92036  L?  binary started by a Windows cmd shell    -> T1059.003 + T1574.001
    Same failure mode as T1082 - the activity is visible but attributed to Execution rather than
    Discovery, and T1016 never appears. Four techniques measured so far, three distinct failure modes:
    parent-only (T1087.001), misattribution (T1082, T1016), partial coverage (T1033).

    TEST SET DELIBERATELY NARROWED. The first T1016 baseline used tests 1/2/4/7/9 and had to be
    discarded: tests 4 (TrickBot Style) and 7 (Qakbot Recon) are multi-technique recon SEQUENCES that
    also run net, whoami and nltest, so custom rules 100200 (T1087.001) and 100240 (T1033) fired
    correctly inside a window labelled T1016 and single-technique attribution became impossible.
    See LABELLING_SCHEME.md 3a. Tests 1, 2 and 9 exercise T1016 alone - confirmed empirically: the
    re-run shows zero 1002xx rules in the attack class.

    PRECISION NOTE ON netsh: `netsh advfirewall ... show` is discovery, but `netsh advfirewall set`
    DISABLES the firewall and is T1562.004 Defense Evasion. 100251 therefore requires an enumeration
    verb. A rule matching netsh broadly would map firewall tampering to Discovery - the same class of
    error this project is measuring in the defaults.
    ===================================================================================================
  -->

  <rule id="100250" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^(ipconfig|arp|route|nbtstat|netstat|nslookup)\.exe$</field>
    <description>T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery via native network utility</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1016</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100251" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^netsh\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\bshow\b</field>
    <description>T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery via netsh enumeration</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1016</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    `net config` reports workstation and server network configuration - T1016, distinct from the account
    enumeration subcommands 100200 matches. The two conditions are disjoint by construction, so a single
    net.exe invocation cannot fire both: 100200 requires user|localgroup|group|accounts, this requires
    config. Verified on the T1016 re-run, where net.exe fired neither 100200 nor anything else.
  -->
  <rule id="100252" level="8">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^net1?\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\s+config\b</field>
    <description>T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery via net config</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1016</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================================
    T1053.005 Scheduled Task
    Custom rule IDs 100270-100271 | level 10

    MEASURED BASELINE (2026-08-08, 4 usable windows, atomics 1/2/4/6/9, 48 attack alerts):
    NOTHING in the default ruleset maps to T1053.005. What fires is:
        92004  -> T1059.003          92027  -> T1059.001
        92032  -> T1087 + T1059.003  92052  -> T1059.003
    Every one is EXECUTION. A Persistence technique is read as Execution - a TACTIC-level miss, and the
    starkest of the seven techniques measured so far. Creating a scheduled task that survives reboot is
    materially different from running a command, and an analyst triaging these alerts would see only
    "powershell spawned powershell" with no indication that anything was left behind.

    LEVEL 10, not 8. Persistence outranks Discovery: a scheduled task grants an attacker return access
    after reboot, whereas host enumeration is reconnaissance. Severity here reflects consequence.

    TWO RULES, split by mechanism:
      100270  schtasks.exe /create or /change  - the native path
      100271  PowerShell and WMI task cmdlets  - Register/Set/New-ScheduledTask, MSFT_ScheduledTask

    PRECISION NOTES:
      * 100270 requires /create or /change. `schtasks /query` is ENUMERATION, not persistence, and the
        benign mirror uses it - matching it would map task listing to Persistence.
      * `schtasks /delete` is deliberately NOT matched. Deleting a task is T1070.009 Indicator Removal,
        a different technique, and it is also what this lab's own cleanup does between runs.
      * 100271 deliberately excludes `Get-ScheduledTask` for the same reason as /query.
    ===================================================================================================
  -->

  <rule id="100270" level="10">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^schtasks\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\s/(create|change)\b</field>
    <description>T1053.005 Scheduled Task created or modified via schtasks</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1053.005</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100271" level="10">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^powershell(\.exe)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(Register-ScheduledTask|Set-ScheduledTask|New-ScheduledTask|MSFT_ScheduledTask)</field>
    <description>T1053.005 Scheduled Task created or modified via PowerShell or WMI</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1053.005</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    ===================================================================================================
    T1136.001 Create Local Account
    Custom rule IDs 100280-100283 | level 10 (100283 level 8)

    NOTE: no literal ampersand appears anywhere in this file. Wazuh parses rule files with its own
    OS_ReadXML, not a standards-compliant parser, and a bare ampersand has been known to fail the load
    even inside a comment. "ATTACK" below means MITRE ATT and CK.

    MEASURED BASELINE (2026-08-08, 5+5 windows, atomics 4/5/8/9, 156 attack alerts):
    a DIFFERENT failure mode from the previous seven - the default ruleset detects the behaviour
    accurately and describes it correctly, then tags it with the WRONG ATTACK technique:

        60109  "User account enabled or created"   -> T1098 Account Manipulation
        60110  "User account changed"              -> T1098
        60111  "User account disabled or deleted"  -> T1098 + T1531
        60154  "Administrators Group Changed"      -> T1484 Domain Policy Modification
        60160  "Domain Users Group Changed"        -> T1484
        60170  "Users Group Changed"               -> T1484

    Rule 60109 fires on Windows Security event 4720, whose literal meaning is "a user account was
    created" - that is T1136.001 by definition. Wazuh maps it to T1098 Account Manipulation, a SIBLING
    technique. And the group rules map local group membership changes to T1484 Domain Policy
    Modification, which is not only the wrong technique but describes a domain mechanism this
    WORKGROUP-only endpoint does not have.

    So this is SIBLING MISATTRIBUTION: same tactic family, adjacent technique, correct description.
    Distinct from T1087.001 (parent-only), T1082/T1016/T1053.005 (wrong tactic entirely) and T1033
    (partial coverage). Arguably the most insidious of the five, because the alert text reads correctly
    and only the ATTACK tag is wrong - so a coverage matrix built from these mappings would show
    T1136.001 as uncovered while showing T1098 and T1484 as covered, both incorrectly.

    APPROACH: 100280 REFINES rather than duplicates. It chains from 60109 via if_sid, so the default
    rule still does the detecting and the custom rule corrects the attribution. That is a different and
    cheaper remediation than writing detection from scratch, and worth contrasting in the write-up with
    T1082/T1016 where no default detection existed to refine.
    ===================================================================================================
  -->

  <rule id="100280" level="10">
    <if_sid>60109</if_sid>
    <description>T1136.001 Local account created (re-attributed from T1098)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1136.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100281" level="10">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^net1?\.exe$</field>
    <!-- Argument order is NOT fixed: the atomic uses `net user /add "name" "pass"` while an
         administrator more often types `net user name pass /add`. Two drafts failed a dry-run against
         both forms before this one - the first required a token between `user` and `/add`, the second
         wrote `\s+user\s.*\s/add` which consumes the single space in `user /add` and then demands a
         second one. Terminate the keyword with `\b`, not `\s`, so the separator stays available.
         `localgroup` cannot match because it lacks the `user` keyword. -->
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\s+user\b.*?\s/add\b</field>
    <description>T1136.001 Local account created via net user /add</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1136.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100282" level="10">
    <if_group>sysmon_eid1_detections</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.originalFileName" type="pcre2">(?i)^powershell(\.exe)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(New-LocalUser|AccountManagement\.UserPrincipal|ADSI.*\bCreate\b.*user)</field>
    <description>T1136.001 Local account created via PowerShell or .NET</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1136.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- 100283: local group membership change. NOT T1136.001 - adding an existing principal to a group is
       T1098 Account Manipulation. Included here because the default rules 60154/60160/60170 tag it
       T1484 Domain Policy Modification, which is wrong on two counts: wrong technique, and a domain
       mechanism this WORKGROUP endpoint does not have. Kept at level 8 (below the creation rules) so
       the ordering of alert severity reflects the ordering of adversary progress. -->
  <rule id="100283" level="8">
    <if_sid>60154, 60160, 60170</if_sid>
    <description>T1098 Local group membership changed (re-attributed from T1484)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1098</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
